    {
      "slug": "in-thee-rejoiceth-poulakis",
      "title": "In Thee Rejoiceth (Poulakis)",
      "year": 1640,
      "imageUrl": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/10/Poulakis_Theodoros_-_The_Hymn_to_the_Virgin%2C_%22E%CF%80%CE%AF_%CE%A3%CE%BF%CE%AF_X%CE%B1%CE%AF%CF%81%CE%B5%CE%B9%22_%28%22In_Thee_Rejoiceth...%22%29_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg",
      "imageWidth": 1744,
      "imageHeight": 2493,
      "story": "In Thee Rejoiceth also known as Epi Si Harri is a tempera and gold leaf painting by Theodore Poulakis. The central figure of the piece is the Virgin Mary. The painting is a tribute to her. Poulakis was from Chania Crete. He was active on the Ionian islands and in Venice during the second half of the 17th century. He was a member of the Cretan school and the father of the Heptanese (Ionian) school. According to the Institute of Neohellenic Research, 130 paintings are attributed to Poulakis.",
      "facts": [
        "Some parts of the work were also inspired by engravings created by the French engraver Étienne Delaune.",
        "The title of the painting is actually All Creation Rejoices in Thee."
      ],
      "wikipediaUrl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/In_Thee_Rejoiceth_(Poulakis)",
